  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Fri Apr 17 2026\n\nConditions today have trended drier, and shower activity has yet\nto begin. PoPs have been trimmed down to minimal slight chance \nalong the northern/western periphery this afternoon. Winds have\nbeen breezy out of the SSW with gusts to around 20-30mph.\nTemperatures have reached the 80s across the entire CWA, with\nnear record highs likely (Cape Girardeau at 86 would seem to\nhave the most likely record to be reached).\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected to develop ahead of a\ncold frontal passage, sweeping across the Quad State tonight\nthrough tomorrow. CAPE drops off after sunset, and shear is\nmarginal, capable of producing a few strong storms. Best chance\nfor a severe storm (with damaging wind as the primary severe\nhazard) would thus be concentrated around midnight in the \nnorthwest, where a D1 SPC slight risk remains. Some thunder \nchances remain through the morning along and ahead of the front \nwith the cold front trailing behind, moving through during the \nmorning and early afternoon hours. Widespread half an inch to \nan inch rainfall is expected with this system.\n\nThe strong cold frontal passage will finally drive temperatures\ndown to near normal for the rest of the weekend and Monday with\nhighs in the 60s to lower 70s and lows near 40. A broad area of\nhigh pressure moving across the country keeps conditions dry \nearly week. Low pressure moving across Ontario may trail a front\nand some light showers down towards the I-64 corridor Tuesday\nnight, but PoPs are only slight chance for now. A warming trend\noccurs midweek as southerly flow returns and ridging moves\nacross the Quad State. An end of week (Friday/Saturday) frontal\npassage appears likely with fairly broad model support.",
